@startuml kernel-message-flow
!theme plain
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center

title How knowledge moves — one pchat message, every hop over the bus

header
<b>DESIGN TARGET — Phase 2.</b> The bus has send() and take() today; call() is not built and will **await**
rather than block (priority inheritance needs CAP_SYS_NICE). Nothing is wired to the bus yet, and
starling-net, pchat, audit and the Persist/Publish effects are not written.
endheader

actor Client
participant "net service" as N
participant "**starling-bus**\n«the only path»" as BUS
participant "pchat\n«feature»" as P
participant "state service\n«single writer»" as S
participant "dyn Permissions\n«starling-model»" as PM
participant "audit\n«feature»" as A
database "storage service" as DB

Client -> N : PluginMessage (TCP)
N -> BUS : send(pchat port, Feature lane)
BUS -> P : deliver
activate P

P -> BUS : call(state port)\n"may user X text in channel Y?"
BUS -> S : deliver **on the caller's lane**
S -> PM : allows(user, channel, TEXT_MESSAGE)
PM --> S : true
S --> BUS : reply(**true**) — an answer, not a reference
BUS --> P : reply(true)

note over S, PM
  **Permissions never left the domain.** ""dyn Permissions""
  computes; the state service only *serves* the question and
  owns the ""Box<dyn Permissions>"". That hop is in-process,
  not a bypass: an evaluator is a pure function owned by the
  service, not a component with a port. The reply carries a
  ""bool"" — a ""&dyn Permissions"" could not cross.
end note

== Effects: routed by target, not funnelled through one service ==

P -> BUS : Persist
BUS -> DB : write-behind
P -> BUS : Publish(MessageStored)
BUS -> A : deliver
deactivate P

activate A
A -> BUS : Persist — audit record
BUS -> DB
deactivate A

P -> BUS : Send { to: Channel(Y) }
BUS -> S : deliver — **needs state to resolve**
activate S
S -> BUS : send(net port) — one per resolved session
deactivate S
BUS -> N : deliver
N -> Client : message

note over BUS, S
  **Only two effects reach the state service.** ""Send"" needs
  the channel and user registries to turn ""Recipients"" into
  sessions (""core/broadcast.rs::resolve""); ""Disconnect"" needs
  the connection registry. ""Persist"", ""Publish"" and ""Log""
  need no state, so the bus takes them straight to their port.

  Funnelling them through the writer would make it a mail hub
  and fail its own admission test — *belongs here only if it
  must be atomic with a state mutation* (""docs/CRATES.md"" §2).
end note

note over P, A
  pchat and audit never call each other, and neither can name
  the state service. They know a ""PortId"" and the bus; the bus
  knows a lane and opaque ""Bytes"".
end note

@enduml
